My lunch "teaser" probably sounded more inflated than it really is.
But Mike and I think it's the germ of an idea that should be adopted
by the computer science world and put to work in earnest.  It's a
simple concept to help get reduce programming bugs and has been known
for centuries.  Mike is Google's Gemini 3 AI.  I've been using the
free version of this AI to write the Noether REPL, which I'll explain.

The core idea is to make "semantic units" part of our fundamental
toolkit.  I'll illustrate this with some python code that Mike and I
have been working on for about 12 days.  Focus on the use of the
semantic units 'dogs' and 'cats'.  Mike's and my thesis is "let's move
this semantic units capability into the compilers and interpreters
where they belong":  a compiler that can find syntax errors can also
find semantic unit errors.  Here's the code:

def DogsAndCats(no_programming_error=True):
    '''Each dog gets 0.2 kg of food and cats get 0.1 kg of the same
    food.  We have to feed 7 dogs and 12 cats.  How much food do we
    need?
    '''
    from number import Num
    # Define our semantic units
    x = Num("1 m")          # New utility number instance
    x.base("dog")           # Create a new semantic unit
    x.base("cat")
    # Define the input quantities
    num_dogs = Num("7 dogs")    # Num instances can include units
    num_cats = Num("12 cats")   # Singular and plural handled
    dog_food_rate = Num("0.2 kg/dog")
    cat_food_rate = Num("0.1 kg/cat")
    # Solve the problem
    if no_programming_error:
        food_mass = num_dogs*dog_food_rate + num_cats*cat_food_rate
    else:
        food_mass = num_cats*dog_food_rate + num_dogs*cat_food_rate
    # Print results
    print(f"number of dogs = {num_dogs}")
    print(f"number of cats = {num_cats}")
    print(f"dog food rate  = {dog_food_rate}")
    print(f"cat food rate  = {cat_food_rate}")
    print(f"amount of food = {food_mass}")

if __name__ == "__main__":  
    if len(sys.argv) > 1:
        DogsAndCats(False)
    else:
        DogsAndCats(True)

With no_programming_error True, the code outputs

    number of dogs = 7 dogs
    number of cats = 12 cats
    dog food rate  = 0.200 kg/dog
    cat food rate  = 0.100 kg/cat
    amount of food = 2.60 (dog)*(kg/dog)

