The exception is telling you the addition barfed because the two
operands don't have equal semantic units:  the first term has
'cats*kg/dog' and the second has 'dogs*kg/cat', which you can see from
inspection of the first non-error output.  An innovation of our Num
class is that you can add new semantic units at any time and the
system learns and persists them, letting you incorporate them into new
expressions.  

The design mirrors how I did calculations with paper and pencil as a
student in the 1960's:  we used semantic units then and we moved
around the numerical domains without friction, not thinking much about
it.  If I remember to attach the file, you'll see how the Num class
colorizes the number types in the terminal output; this is handy to
keep types straight in your mind.

This is being run in vanilla python 3.11.  The Num object is an
abstract representation of a number that can carry semantic units.
The deep idea is that the numerical information and the semantic unit
combine to make a "Noether invariant".  This tips the hat to Emmy
Noether's famous first theorem, popular with physics, which relates
symmetries to things that are conserved, i.e., invariants.  If you
want to go study the original context, you'll get bogged down in the
calculus of variations and Lagrangians.  But here's the gist.  (The
pedants will trot out things like "free abelian groups" to describe
units, but I'm not sure that's quite right either.)  Engineers can
think about the units for a problem as analogous to a vector in a
vector space.  For velocity in m/s, for example, the "basis vectors"
are length L and time T, often displayed as L·T⁻¹.  When talking about
invariance, you also need transformations under which things are
invariant.  Here, the transformations are scaling by constant factors
(what we used to call dilatations), such as by statements like 'one
meter is the same as 3.28 feet'.

Thus, Num("1 m/s") is a Noether invariant.  You can think of it like
the angular momentum of a tire when you see a snapshot of car driving
away.  That abstract angular momentum vector would be sticking out
from the center of the wheel if you could see it.  You can move
anywhere in space and that abstract vector points to the same point in
space.  If you look at it in a mirror it has transform in a certain
way (pseudovector) to still look like the same vector.

The Noether invariant Num("1 m/s") is similar to the angular momentum
vector.  If you impose a scaling transformation to feet on the meter
part of the unit, the Noether invariant's representation has to change
to Num("3.28 ft") for it to remain the same thing (i.e., invariant).
Note that the word "representation" is being used in the same sense as
the concept of e.g. "using a 3x3 matrix to represent an element of the
abstract SO(3) group", covered by representation theory in math.
Thus, there's an implied algebraic structure.  

Now, it's a short drive to computer science.  Let's change "Noether
invariant" to "type".  Our thesis is simply that the number and
semantic unit together should be a single type.  Another important
idea is that these different semantic units are "orthogonal" to each
other (again, the vector space analogy), giving the concept of type
its real teeth.

I'd like to see every compiler and interpreter on Earth handle these
semantic units, as we've stripped away the units and virtually all
programming languages deal with naked floats.  At best we're left with
variable names like speed_m_per_s but there's no checking machinery
except the overworked underpaid programmer, who nearly always misses
it.  I'd imagine the bug rate in software would decrease if these
semantic type errors wouldn't get past the compiler/interpreter.

